# 项目开发规则 / Project development rules

此文件是唯一共享模板。中英文表达相同规则和字段；填值保持一致，流程只执行一次。两种表述若有实质冲突，先澄清，不自行扩大权限。

This is the single shared template. Both languages express the same rules and fields; keep filled-in values consistent and run the workflow once. Clarify any substantive discrepancy before proceeding; do not expand permissions yourself.

## 简体中文

### 项目开发规则

环境默认（可按项目配置）：云电脑优先；用户指定环境或任务依赖本地资源时选择合适的授权环境。优先已连接 API/连接器，不为绕过访问拒绝或安全限制而切换路径。开工前一次集中申请端到端所需的全部权限；密钥与凭据不落 agent 工作机。

采用模板版本：<VERSION / COMMIT>（workflow v2.1）。项目负责人：<ROLE>。

1. 读 README、docs/workflow-v2.md（workflow v2.1）、相关 spec 与运行说明。
2. 检查 git status，保留其他修改；只提交本任务文件。
3. 按 workflow v2.1 完成规格、失败复现、实现、回归与独立评审。每批改动按「每批闭环与分级处理」执行：S1 或测试不过直接返工；S2 修后复测复评；S3 与低风险项按评审决策推进。
4. 分工与模型：独立评审 <REVIEWER_OR_SERVICE>；编码 <CODING_TOOL_AND_TIER>；技术研究 <RESEARCH_ROLE>（项目没有配置时，由负责编程的 agent 自己查官方文档并附来源链接）。启动设计、评审或编码前确认能选到指定模型与档位，选不到就停下回报，不自行换模型或降档。技术问题查官方原文并附链接，不凭印象作答；卡住时先穷尽自身手段，再附证据上报。
5. 测试命令：<UNIT>、<INTEGRATION>、<LINT>、<SECURITY>；未配置项明确标记。
6. 合并策略：<PROJECT_APPROVED_STRATEGY>；保护与 required checks：<LIST>。
7. 提交署名：先核实已有授权和工具能力，不把命令行未登录误判为连接器无权限。真实记录 author/committer 与 AI 来源；工具支持且项目允许时用 AI 作者名称。工具不能指定 AI author 且项目允许时，保留实际连接账户身份，提交明确 AI authored 及 Agent trailer，PR 披露实际署名与限制。强制身份/DCO/签名无法满足时停止请示，不绕过。人工保留选定身份，不强制 noreply。trailer 键名与格式照抄 <ATTRIBUTION_SCRIPT_OR_POLICY> 原文；项目没有署名脚本或策略文件时，另加来源 trailer：云端运行（如 Codex 云端）只写工具标识 `Code-Tool: <工具> <模型> <档位>`，不写 Host-Id；本地电脑运行写 `Code-Tool: <工具> <模型> <档位>` 并加 `Host-Id: <host-id>`（文档只用占位符；本地运行时 host id 会出现在公开的提交记录里）。详见 workflow-v2 署名规则。
8. 批准与停止点：重大变更、对外发布、部署、凭据及权限变更分别取得所需批准。此外，合并、部署、改写已推送历史、凭据、真实隐私命中、政策文件、新增依赖、超出已批准范围和重大取舍都须停下等用户批准（完整说明见 workflow v2.1）。每次合并评审在先；Draft PR 由人决定转 ready 和合并。不绕过保护、不强推、不跳过失败检查。
9. 推送与发布前单独运行隐私与凭据扫描，扫描与推送分两条命令；任何命中（含疑似误报）先停下上报。已关闭 PR 的分支不删除。
10. 不将个人助手、设备或生活分工写入项目文件与 PR。不提交凭据、私人日志、真实个人邮箱正文或原始资料导出。

## English

### Project development rules

Environment default (configurable per project): prefer the cloud computer; follow a user-specified environment or use a suitable authorized environment for required local resources. Prefer connected APIs/connectors; never switch paths to evade denied access or security restrictions. Request every permission the end-to-end task needs in one batch before starting; keys and credentials never land on the agent's work machine.

Adopted template version: <VERSION / COMMIT> (workflow v2.1). Project owner: <ROLE>.

1. Read README, docs/workflow-v2.md (workflow v2.1), relevant specs, and run instructions.
2. Check git status and preserve other changes; commit only files belonging to this task.
3. Follow workflow v2.1 for specification, failure reproduction, implementation, regression testing, and independent review. Handle each batch under "Per-batch loop and severity handling": S1 or failing tests go straight back for rework; S2 is fixed, retested, and re-reviewed; S3 and low-risk items proceed per the review decision.
4. Roles and models: independent review <REVIEWER_OR_SERVICE>; coding <CODING_TOOL_AND_TIER>; technical research <RESEARCH_ROLE> (if the project has not configured one, the agent responsible for coding checks the official documentation itself and attaches source links). Before starting design, review, or coding, confirm that the specified model and tier can be selected; if not, stop and report, and do not switch models or lower the tier on your own. Answer technical questions from the official source with links, not from memory; when stuck, exhaust your own options before escalating with evidence.
5. Test commands: <UNIT>, <INTEGRATION>, <LINT>, <SECURITY>; explicitly mark unconfigured items.
6. Merge strategy: <PROJECT_APPROVED_STRATEGY>; protections and required checks: <LIST>.
7. Commit attribution: verify existing access and tool capabilities first; a CLI without a login does not imply missing connector access. Record author/committer and AI origin truthfully; use an AI author name when supported and permitted. If the tool cannot set an AI author and project rules allow it, retain the actual connected-account identity, state AI authored and an Agent trailer in the commit, and disclose actual attribution and limitations in the PR. Stop and ask when mandatory identity/DCO/signing rules cannot be met; do not bypass them. Humans retain their chosen identity without mandatory noreply. Copy trailer keys and format verbatim from <ATTRIBUTION_SCRIPT_OR_POLICY>; if the project has no attribution script or policy file, add source trailers: when running in the cloud (such as Codex cloud), only the tool identifier `Code-Tool: <tool> <model> <tier>`, with no Host-Id; when running on a local computer, `Code-Tool: <tool> <model> <tier>` plus `Host-Id: <host-id>` (documents use only placeholders; for local runs the host id appears in the public commit history). See workflow-v2 attribution rules.
8. Approvals and stop points: obtain the required approvals separately for major changes, external releases, deployments, credentials, and permission changes. In addition, merging, deployment, rewriting pushed history, credentials, real privacy hits, policy files, new dependencies, work beyond the approved scope, and major trade-offs must stop and wait for user approval (details in workflow v2.1). Every merge is reviewed first; a person decides when a Draft PR becomes ready and whether it is merged. Do not bypass protections, force-push, or skip failing checks.
9. Before every push and release, run the privacy and credential scan on its own; scanning and pushing are two separate commands. Stop and report on any hit, including a suspected false positive. Do not delete the branches of closed PRs.
10. Do not put personal-assistant, device, or personal-life responsibilities in project files or PRs. Do not commit credentials, private logs, real personal email addresses in body text, or raw source-material exports.
